> 
> With the assumption that all of the tests ran and the listed failures are exhaustive, I see two ways forward:
> 1. The "reasonably certain" option I think is to remove `Sdl.Window.State.OpenGL` and use the temporary window solution all of the time, instead of just as an emergency fallback. This was what I recently changed after @ThomasFOG 's message in July.
> 2. The solution I am not entirely certain will even fix it is to add `[RunOnUI]` to all of the test fixtures that are currently failing, but in addition to not being certain this will fix it, I could see how this could incur additional test environment costs by increasing test runtime since only the UI thread can run them.
> 
> Which of the two options is preferred, or is there another path forward that I am not thinking of?
...
> So looking at the new failures, the Linux ones are still failing with:
> > `Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Graphics.NoSuitableGraphicsDeviceException : Could not load EGL library`
> 
> The MacOS failures are now:
> > `Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Graphics.NoSuitableGraphicsDeviceException : NSWindow should only be instantiated on the main thread!`
> 
> The latter seems pretty strange given that I had added `[RunOnUI]`.
> 
> I have gone and pushed a revert of the OpenGL flag change and the `[RunOnUI]` change to see if it improves the test results.
...
> That revert fixes the test failures on Linux, but the `NSWindow should only be instantiated on the main thread!` persists on MacOS.
> 
> I suspect that Linux was failing because the OpenGL initialization was possibly pushed too earlier, or maybe whatever is causing the MacOS failures contributed.
> 
> I do not see any failure logs for the `GraphicsDeviceManager` tests, and I do not see anything that would _exclude_ them from only MacOS, so I suspect they do run there and do pass, so it is a matter of figuring out what the difference is between those passing tests, and the tests that now fail because of the error checking/throw that I added to surface more accurate errors to developers so they do not need to spelunk through MonoGame to find platform issues like this PR is trying to fix.
> 
> I see two differences:
> - `[RunOnUI]` was added on the fixture class as well, not just the methods. I did not include this because I figured the tests themselves did not need that, only `SetUp` (and possibly `TearDown`).
> - I also see `[NonParallelizable]`, but given the error message complaining about not being on the main thread it does not seem as related.
> 
> `[NonParallelizable]` can also be added to methods (according to the attribute usage flags anyway), so I am going to try adding that and `[RunOnUI]` to the failing `SetUp` method (and its `TearDown` sibling).
> 
> I do not have an environment to reproduce the tests myself in unfortunately, and since these ideas are mostly just shots in the dark I am interested in any input to reduce the guesswork.
...
> So, on mac any "window" method calls need to be called on the main thread, when running nunit tests the tests are run on background threads, this is what causes the `NSWindow` issue for any test which does anything with the window. 
> See https://github.com/MonoGame/MonoGame/blob/develop/Tests/Runner/Desktop/RunOnUIAttribute.cs#L26. 
> 
> Now I'm not sure if adding `RunOnUI` to setup methods will work. Because that class only seems to work on "TestCommand", I don't know if that covers Setup methods.
...
> So after further inspection since nothing seemed to work, I realized that the `NSWindow should only be instantiated on the main thread!` message was not the exception causing the test failure (directly anyway), the exception causing the test failure was because in `TearDown()` `Dispose()` was being called on `_game` while it was `null` because of the `NSWindow should only be instantiated on the main thread!` exception being thrown in `SetUp()` causing it to never get set.
