        _graphicsDeviceManager = new GraphicsDeviceManager(_game) { GraphicsProfile = GraphicsProfile.HiDef };
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop) { Name = "GraphicsDeviceFixture", IsBackground = true };
        _thread.Start();
        _initialized.Wait();
        _initializationFailure?.Throw(); // hmm, wrap it
    }
    ...
}
```

Wait: creating `new GraphicsDeviceManager(_game)` on the caller thread, but device creation happens on the fixture thread — the GDM constructor adds itself to services; ApplyChanges on the fixture thread creates the window + device there. The probe was doing everything on the main thread. GDM construction off-thread should be fine (just bookkeeping); ApplyChanges does the GL work → on the fixture thread. But to be safest, do GDM construction inside the initialization work item on the fixture thread too. Let's do that — put all MonoGame object creation inside the loop thread.

RunLoop:
```csharp
private void RunLoop()
{
    try
    {
        var gdm = new GraphicsDeviceManager(_game) { GraphicsProfile = GraphicsProfile.HiDef };
        gdm.ApplyChanges();
        _graphicsDeviceManager = gdm;
        GraphicsDevice = gdm.GraphicsDevice;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        _initializationFailure = ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex);
    }
    finally { _initialized.Set(); }
    foreach (var work in _workQueue.GetConsumingEnumerable())
    {
        try { work(); } catch { /* already captured on the Run side */ }
    }
    _game.Dispose(); // dispose gdm + device
}
```

Hmm, exception handling: the work action itself should capture the exception and rethrow it on the caller. Pattern:

```csharp
public void Run(Action action)
{
    ExceptionDispatchInfo? failure = null;
    using var done = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    _workQueue.Add(() =>
    {
        try { action(); } catch (Exception ex) { failure = ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex); } finally { done.Set(); }
    });
    done.Wait();
    failure?.Throw();
}

public T Run<T>(Func<T> func)
{
    T result = default!;
    Run(() => result = func());
    return result;
}
```

Does ManualResetEventSlim need Dispose? It has Dispose but only if you use AvailableWaitHandle... the `using var done` inside the lambda captured after Set — done gets disposed when Run returns, no problem.

Initialization failure semantics: the constructor should throw with a friendly message:

```csharp
if (_initializationFailure != null)
    throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to create GraphicsDevice: please check the display environment (on desktopless environments you need /dev/dri, or use xvfb-run).", ...)
```
But ExceptionDispatchInfo.Throw rethrows the original exception; better to wrap: throw new InvalidOperationException(msg, _initializationFailure.SourceException). Also dispose the thread? If initialization fails, tests can't run anyway; still, clean up: _workQueue.CompleteAdding.

Wait — if initialization fails, the thread proceeds to the consuming loop; CompleteAdding in the constructor's catch path, then let it join? The fixture constructor throws → xUnit treats all tests as failed. Cleanup is optional but polite.

Also, if Run is called after Dispose / during a failure — ObjectDisposedException from BlockingCollection.Add; acceptable.

GraphicsDevice property: `public GraphicsDevice GraphicsDevice { get; private set; }` — non-null after successful constructor. Nullable annotation: either use `= null!` or make it a private set with null-forgiving.

HostGame: `private sealed class HostGame : Game;` — no members needed since Game's constructor is public and Initialize is never called.

Dispose:
```csharp
public void Dispose()
{
    _workQueue.CompleteAdding();
    _thread.Join();
    _workQueue.Dispose(); _initialized.Dispose();
}
```
_game.Dispose() is after the loop inside RunLoop — disposes GDM + device on the fixture thread. Good.

One concern: disposal order — GraphicsDeviceManager.Dispose disposes the device. Game.Dispose also disposes components/services? MonoGame's Game.Dispose disposes the GDM if it's a IDisposable in services... anyway, it's fine; the probe didn't dispose the game at all (it used `using var game`). Should I also call gdm.Dispose? Game.Dispose handles it (MonoGame's Game.Dispose → disposes _graphicsDeviceManager?). PR #5728: "calling game.Dispose() allows the GraphicsDeviceManager to be cleaned up properly" — so game.Dispose() is the correct cleanup. Good.

