Reviewing the comments one by one:

L9 "Process-level shared graphics device fixture" — "fixture" is xUnit terminology, fine if aimed at developers with foundational background; "process-level shared" is also OK. Could expand a bit more: create one GraphicsDevice within the test process, shared across all tests. Better to make it self-contained.

L13-14 "The window remains hidden per MonoGame's design" — vague, replace with something specific: SDL windows are created with the hidden flag from the start, and only Game.Run() displays them; this fixture does not call Run, so no visible window occurs.

L15-17 "Directly constructing GraphicsDevice is not possible on DesktopGL — GraphicsAdapter.CurrentDisplayMode depends on SdlGameWindow.Instance which is only assigned in the Game construction flow" — "only assigned in the Game construction flow" — actually SdlGameWindow.Instance is assigned in the SdlGameWindow constructor, which happens when the GraphicsDeviceManager creates the window. If you construct GraphicsDevice directly, no SdlGameWindow gets created at all, and Instance is null. Phrasing: CurrentDisplayMode internally reads SdlGameWindow.Instance.Handle, and that instance is only created in the Game/GraphicsDeviceManager flow; if you bypass Game and directly new GraphicsDevice, Instance is null and NullReferenceException is thrown. Also the issue reference "(MonoGame issue #5089)" is kept — useful.

L20 "The threading model follows MonoGame official tests' approach" — what is "official tests' approach"? Unclear to the reader. Replace with the facts: OpenGL contexts are bound to the thread that created them (current context semantics), and rendering calls across threads are undefined behavior. MonoGame's own test suite also consolidates device operations onto a single thread. Therefore this fixture launches its own dedicated thread… "Marshalling" — is that jargon? It's a standard concurrency term (marshalling), but the Chinese "編組" is a literal translation; developers might understand "編組"… more natural would be: "submit to the dedicated thread for execution". Use plain wording: consolidate onto a dedicated thread for execution.

L22 is fine but "submit work" is OK.

L25-26 "Marshalling only guarantees thread affinity, does not isolate device state (e.g. current RenderTarget)" — mostly clear. Rephrase: work submitted by multiple callers is executed serially on the same thread, but GraphicsDevice state (SetRenderTarget etc.) is globally shared; when two tests interleave submission, each sees the state changes made by the other. Therefore test projects using this fixture should disable test case parallelization.

L29-30 consumer-side registration approach — "consumer side" is fine, but could also be "usage in test projects". Keep the code snippet. The AssemblyFixture attribute exists in xUnit v3 — check the name: xUnit v3 has AssemblyFixtureAttribute in the Xunit namespace, yes. IAssemblyFixture<T> also exists. OK.

L51-52 exception message — "should be directly creatable if there's a desktop session" OK; "in environments without a desktop session, /dev/dri must be available (KMSDRM/offscreen), otherwise tests should be launched with xvfb-run" — passable. Could clarify: requires a desktop session (X11/Wayland), or a directly accessible GPU node (/dev/dri, SDL uses KMSDRM/offscreen backend), or launch via xvfb-run. Improve it.

L60 "Can only be safely used within work submitted via Run" — OK. But note: the GraphicsDevice property itself can be read from any thread (just a reference); use must be done inside Run. Fine as is, but could sharpen: the reference can be read from any thread, but all device operations must be performed within the work submitted by Run.

L93 "Must be a statement block lambda: an expression lambda with a return value will rebind to Run<T> and cause infinite recursion" — clarify: `() => result = func()` is an expression lambda whose value can convert to Func<T>, and overload resolution will preferentially select Run<T> rather than Run(Action), causing self-recursion. Rewrite: Here we must use a statement block lambda: the assignment expression `() => result = func()` is convertible to Func<T>, and overload resolution will bind it back to Run<T> itself, causing infinite recursion (already happened). Maybe drop the "already happened" part.

